Public key encryption with keyword search (PEKS) is a method to search over encrypted data in the cloud. However, traditional PEKS schemes are based on classical mathematical problems, which are vulnerable to quantum attacks. To address the issues, several authors proposed lattice-based PEKS. Although lattice-based PEKS resist quantum attacks, they are inefficient and also suffer from certificate management problem (CMP) or key escrow problem (KEP). To address these issues, in this paper, we propose module lattice-based certificateless encryption with keyword search (ML-CLEKS) without trapdoor algorithms. The security of ML-CLEKS is proved against TYPE-I and TYPE-II adversaries based on Module Learning with Error (Module LWE) in random oracle model. Theoretical analysis and experimental results demonstrate that ML-CLEKS is efficient.